	abstract = {Accurate day-ahead electricity price forecasting is a fundamental aspect of efficient energy market management, particularly in regions characterized by high volatility, such as the Danish DK1 electricity market. This study proposes and evaluates a hybrid deep learning framework designed to enhance the reliability and precision of electricity price predictions. The hybrid architecture synergistically integrates Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) to automatically extract local and spatial features from historical price data, a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) to generate compact, regularized representations thereby mitigating noise and strengthening model robustness against sudden price spikes, and Gated Recurrent Units (GRU) to capture complex temporal dependencies and sequential patterns. To assess the performance of this framework, it is compared with several benchmark models, including traditional Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs), standalone CNNs, GRUs, and CNN-GRU combinations. Model evaluation is conducted using standard performance metrics, such as Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Mean Squared Error (MSE), and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), under various market conditions. The results demonstrate that the proposed hybrid model consistently outperforms benchmark approaches, achieving superior prediction accuracy and generalization capacity. These findings suggest that integrating CNN, VAE, and GRU techniques provides a practical and effective solution for forecasting day-ahead electricity prices in highly dynamic and volatile energy markets.  © 2026 IEEE.},
